From 15766490a0877ae525d08229aa2472e9391b3eef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 17:41:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Widen the type-feedback and specializing-tier censuses past
 their seven suites
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Item 2-13 said this engine's largest private deficiency is zlib, 12.0x behind
Jint relative to Chromium. Asking what the censuses say about zlib turned up
that they say nothing: TypeFeedbackMetrics, SpecializingTierMetrics and
PropertyMapDistributionMetrics all ran the same seven suites, and the roadmap
quotes their output as "the corpus" — the phrase every phase-3 and phase-4
headline uses — over a denominator it never states.

Why it was seven had not been written down, and is not a choice about cost.
Widening the list aborts at the ninth suite with an uncatchable .NET stack
overflow in Mandreel's global_init. Item 0-2's 16 MiB thread and 4 MiB reserve
are a property of Broiler.JavaScript/Program.cs — the shell — and the benchmark
hosts built plain contexts on whatever stack the runtime handed Main. Every
benchmark host in this campaign has been running without the reserve the shell
has had since phase 0. Both censuses also serialized once at the end, so that
abort discarded every suite before it.

Fixed for both: they run on the shell's thread with the shell's
MaxStackUsageBytes, and checkpoint after every suite. Mandreel now fails the way
the shell makes it fail, catchably. TypeFeedbackMetrics also emits the corpus
aggregate with its suite count beside it, rather than leaving it to be totalled
by whoever quotes it — which is the step where the suite list stopped travelling
with the number.

Both widened counts move a headline, and both reproduce their old value over the
old seven, so each is the same instrument over more suites.

  reads, monomorphic:  93.54% of 37.9 M  ->  80.11% of 307.9 M
  calls, monomorphic:  96.70% of 4.24 M  ->  86.35% of 52.9 M
  boxes allocated:        31,401,346     ->     90,641,738
  allocated bytes:           3.13 GB     ->       12.93 GB

87.7% of the corpus's property reads and 65.4% of its boxes were outside the
seven. Gameboy — never in a census — is 34.60% monomorphic with 1 282
polymorphic sites and allocates 41.3 M boxes on its own, 1.32x the whole
previously measured corpus. Splay is 10.15% monomorphic. Typescript is 67% of
the corpus's reads and allocates 4.96 GB.

0090's GC denominator survives the widening: collection is 1.80% of the wider
corpus against 2.29% of the seven.

zlib, Mandreel and RegExp still report setup-only counts for three pre-existing
reasons — the `read` builtin lives in the shell, Mandreel now hits the guard,
RegExp has its checksum defect — so both headlines are over 12 suites and the
output says so.

